Verify the pull request for https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102 at its head commit. Check out the PR head and run every command below from the repository root. First run `npm ci` so the workspace installs from the lockfile. Do one check per acceptance line, in order, and report PASS or FAIL for each with the evidence (the command output).

Check 1. Expect line: "packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> prints <file>:<line>: <error> for every row that is not valid JSON (1-based lines, blank lines skipped, an unterminated last line checked) and exits 1 if any, 0 if none"
Run:
```
d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{"a":1}\n\n[1,2]\n' > "$d/clean.jsonl"; printf '{"a":1}\n{bad\n\n{"b":2}\n{"c":' > "$d/bad.jsonl"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js "$d/clean.jsonl"; echo "exit=$?"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js "$d/bad.jsonl" 2>&1; echo "exit=$?"; echo "dir=$d"
```
PASS if all of these hold:
- The clean-file run prints nothing before its `exit=0`.
- The bad-file run prints exactly two report lines and then `exit=1`. The first starts with `<dir>/bad.jsonl:2: ` and the second with `<dir>/bad.jsonl:5: `, where `<dir>` is the printed `dir=` value. Each line has a non-empty error text after `: `.
- No line is reported for line 3 (blank) or for lines 1 and 4 (valid JSON).
FAIL if:
- any of the above differs, or
- line 5 (the unterminated last row) is missing, or
- the line numbers are off (not 1-based, or blank lines not counted in the numbering), or
- the output format is not `<file>:<line>: <error>`, or
- either exit code is wrong.

Check 2. Expect line: "A missing file or no argument prints to stderr and exits 2"
Run:
```
node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js /nonexistent/missing.jsonl >/tmp/jl-out.txt 2>/tmp/jl-err.txt; echo "exit=$?"; echo "stdout:"; cat /tmp/jl-out.txt; echo "stderr:"; cat /tmp/jl-err.txt; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js >/tmp/jl-out.txt 2>/tmp/jl-err.txt; echo "exit=$?"; echo "stdout:"; cat /tmp/jl-out.txt; echo "stderr:"; cat /tmp/jl-err.txt
```
PASS if both invocations show `exit=2` and some non-empty text under `stderr:`.
FAIL if either exit code is not 2, or either stderr is empty. An uncaught Node stack trace with exit 1 is a FAIL.

Check 3. Expect line: "packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js exports lint(text) returning [{ line, error }]"
Run:
```
node --input-type=module -e "import { lint } from './packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(lint('{\"a\":1}\n{bad\n\n{\"c\":')), JSON.stringify(lint('{\"a\":1}\n\n[2]\n')))"
```
PASS if the import succeeds and the output has two parts:
- First: a JSON array of exactly two objects, each with a `line` number and a non-empty string `error`, with `line` values 2 and 4 in that order.
- Second: `[]`.
FAIL if:
- the import fails, or there is no named export `lint`, or
- the result is not an array of `{ line, error }` objects, or
- the line numbers differ, or
- the clean input does not give `[]`.

Check 4. Expect line: "The workspace has its own package.json (jsonl-lint, type module, test script node --test), no runtime dependencies, and node:test tests for a clean file, a bad middle row, a torn last row, blank lines and the exit codes"
Run:
```
node -e "const p=require('./packages/jsonl-lint/package.json'); console.log(JSON.stringify({name:p.name,type:p.type,test:p.scripts&&p.scripts.test,dependencies:p.dependencies||null}))"; ls packages/jsonl-lint/test; npm test -w jsonl-lint; echo "exit=$?"
```
and
```
grep -rnE "\b(test|it|describe)\(" packages/jsonl-lint --include=*.js; echo "exit=$?"
```
Then read the test files the grep lists.
PASS if all of these hold:
- The JSON shows `"name":"jsonl-lint"`, `"type":"module"`, and a `test` value of `node --test` (extra flags or file arguments after `node --test` are fine).
- `dependencies` is `null` or `{}`.
- `npm test -w jsonl-lint` reports all tests passing with `exit=0`.
- The test files import from `node:test`, and between them cover each of: a clean file, a bad middle row, a torn or unterminated last row, blank lines, and the CLI exit codes. Reading the test bodies is enough to confirm this.
FAIL if any field is wrong, there are runtime dependencies, any test fails or none run, the tests don't use `node:test`, or any of the five listed cases has no test.

Check 5. Expect line: "packages/jsonl-lint/README.md exists and the root README lists the tool"
Run:
```
test -f packages/jsonl-lint/README.md && echo "README present"; grep -n "jsonl-lint" README.md
```
PASS if it prints `README present` and at least one root `README.md` line that names `jsonl-lint` as a tool in the repo.
FAIL if the package README is missing or the root README does not mention `jsonl-lint`.

